    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d11">

                <pb facs="0076" n="26" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv12/pg26"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Paris Peace Conf. 862.00/172: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Garrett</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Commission to Negotiate
                            Peace</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >The Hague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-02-16T13:00:00+00:19">February 16, 1919—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 17—2:30 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>157. Daily report. Baron von Maltzan, Counselor of German Legation at The Hague
                    has sent word through intermediary that Count <pb facs="0077" n="27"
                        xml:id="pg27"/> Brockdorff-Rantzau in framing future policy considers it to
                    be of first importance that he learn American opinion regarding his speech
                    summarized in my No. 155.<anchor n="5c" xml:id="d11-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Garrett</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d11-f1" n="5c" xml:id="d11-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. Translation of speech printed <hi rendition="#italic"
